# Line-ending policy.
#
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true, so a Windows clone rewrites every
# LF to CRLF on checkout. For anything with a shebang that is fatal rather than
# cosmetic: the kernel reads the interpreter path up to the newline, so `#!/usr/bin/env
# bash\r` asks for an interpreter literally named "bash\r" and WSL2 answers
#
#     bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M: no such file or directory
#
# before a single line of the script runs. The same CRLF also breaks `case` patterns,
# heredoc terminators and any `read`-into-variable comparison, so a script that
# survives its shebang can still fail in ways that look like logic bugs.
#
# Forcing eol=lf on everything the platform interprets makes a Windows clone behave
# identically to a macOS or Linux one. This is checkout policy, so it only takes
# effect on a fresh clone or after `git add --renormalize .`.

# Default: let Git decide what is text, but never rewrite line endings on checkout.
*               text=auto eol=lf

# --- Executed directly (shebang matters most here) --------------------------------
*.sh            text eol=lf
leco-cli.sh     text eol=lf
tools/claude-plugin/bin/leco-mcp-launch     text eol=lf
tools/claude-plugin/bin/validate-skills     text eol=lf

# --- Interpreted or parsed (CRLF is silently corrupting rather than fatal) ---------
*.py            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.env           text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf

# --- Copied into containers verbatim ----------------------------------------------
infra/runtimes/**/entrypoint.sh             text eol=lf
ecosystem-stack/**/*.sh                     text eol=lf

# --- Never touch: byte-exact content ----------------------------------------------
*.pem           binary
*.crt           binary
*.key           binary
